WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.133
The simple counting sort

00:00:02.209 --> 00:00:05.493
writes back bare keys: key 2, 4 times.

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:07.402
But usually each element

00:00:07.478 --> 00:00:09.235
is a record with a key

00:00:09.235 --> 00:00:11.449
a student with a grade, say

00:00:11.449 --> 00:00:14.580
and we must move the records themselves.

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:18.219
The standard version, from the textbook

00:00:18.219 --> 00:00:20.933
turns the counts into positions.

00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:24.222
First, count the records with each key.

00:00:24.222 --> 00:00:26.114
Then take prefix sums

00:00:26.196 --> 00:00:28.827
each count becomes the number of

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:31.130
records with key at most k.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.587
Then scan the input from right to left

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.137
and put each record at position

00:00:37.216 --> 00:00:38.571
count of its key

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:41.200
after taking one off that count.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.895
Here are 7 cards with keys 0

00:00:44.895 --> 00:00:46.020
1 and 2

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:49.395
labelled a to g so we can follow them.

00:00:49.481 --> 00:00:52.077
The program in the notes sorts

00:00:52.164 --> 00:00:54.500
them with m equal to 3.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.020
Counting gives 2, 2, 3.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.598
Prefix sums give 2, 4, 7

00:01:01.598 --> 00:01:05.688
keys 0 go to positions 0 and 1

00:01:05.688 --> 00:01:08.449
keys 1 to 2 and 3

00:01:08.449 --> 00:01:12.130
and keys 2 to 4, 5 and 6.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.126
Now the output array

00:01:14.126 --> 00:01:16.400
and the scan from the right.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:20.024
1g: count of 1 drops to 3

00:01:20.024 --> 00:01:23.111
so 1g goes to position 3.

00:01:23.111 --> 00:01:26.291
2f: count of 2 drops to 6.

00:01:26.291 --> 00:01:28.630
0e: to position 1.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.447
1d: position 2.

00:01:31.447 --> 00:01:34.271
2c: position 5.

00:01:34.271 --> 00:01:37.223
0b: position 0.

00:01:37.223 --> 00:01:40.560
And 2a: position 4.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:44.070
The three 2s come out as 2a, 2c

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.189
2f: their input order.

00:01:46.271 --> 00:01:49.857
Scanning from the right puts the last record

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:53.280
with a key in the last slot for that key

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.970
so this counting sort is stable.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.744
In Java, the three steps are three loops.

00:01:59.744 --> 00:02:00.990
Count the keys.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:02.671
Prefix sums

00:02:02.671 --> 00:02:07.660
count[k] += count[k - 1].

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.476
Then the loop from the last

00:02:10.568 --> 00:02:13.582
index down to 0: take the key

00:02:13.582 --> 00:02:15.500
decrement its count

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.610
and copy the record to that position of out.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.648
Time O(n + m)

00:02:21.648 --> 00:02:24.348
and extra space O(n + m)

00:02:24.348 --> 00:02:27.470
for the counts and the output array.

00:02:27.554 --> 00:02:29.664
Stability is exactly what

00:02:29.748 --> 00:02:31.520
RadixSort will need.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.731
A common slip is to scan from left

00:02:34.808 --> 00:02:36.820
to right in the last loop.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.835
The keys still come out sorted

00:02:39.835 --> 00:02:43.531
but equal keys are reversed: 2f, 2c

00:02:43.531 --> 00:02:44.133
2a.

00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:46.712
The sort is no longer stable

00:02:46.712 --> 00:02:49.205
and RadixSort, built on it

00:02:49.205 --> 00:02:51.010
gives wrong answers.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.238
Counting sort needs integer keys.

00:02:54.238 --> 00:02:56.966
BucketSort handles real numbers

00:02:56.966 --> 00:02:59.374
under a different assumption

00:02:59.454 --> 00:03:02.343
the values are spread uniformly over

00:03:02.423 --> 00:03:04.830
the interval from 0 to 1.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.523
With n values

00:03:06.523 --> 00:03:10.551
divide the interval into n equal buckets.

00:03:10.551 --> 00:03:14.003
A value x goes to bucket n * x

00:03:14.003 --> 00:03:15.346
rounded down.

00:03:15.346 --> 00:03:16.976
With 10 values

00:03:16.976 --> 00:03:20.620
that is just the first decimal digit.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.735
0.78 goes to bucket 7

00:03:24.735 --> 00:03:27.727
0.17 to bucket 1

00:03:27.727 --> 00:03:30.334
0.39 to 3.

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:32.554
Then 2, 7, 9.

00:03:32.554 --> 00:03:35.353
Then 2, 1, 2, and 6.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:37.863
Each value is appended to

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:39.890
the end of its list.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.270
The buckets are already in order

00:03:43.364 --> 00:03:47.063
every value in bucket j is smaller than

00:03:47.158 --> 00:03:50.287
every value in bucket j + 1.

00:03:50.382 --> 00:03:52.564
But inside a bucket the

00:03:52.658 --> 00:03:56.736
values are not sorted, so we sort each one.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:04:00.340
Bucket 1, bucket 2, bucket 7.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.349
Finally, list the buckets in order

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:04.793
from 0 to 9.

00:04:04.793 --> 00:04:06.312
With uniform input

00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:08.971
each bucket holds about one value

00:04:08.971 --> 00:04:11.250
so the sorting step is cheap.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:14.396
In the notes' Java version

00:04:14.396 --> 00:04:17.437
distribute makes n empty lists

00:04:17.437 --> 00:04:21.333
and adds each value x to list n * x

00:04:21.333 --> 00:04:23.234
cast to an integer.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:26.179
Then BucketSort walks through

00:04:26.274 --> 00:04:28.270
the buckets in order

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.940
sorts each one with a small insertion sort

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.260
and copies its values back into the array.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.625
On the slide example it prints

00:04:37.692 --> 00:04:39.640
exactly the buckets we built

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.050
and the sorted list.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:45.248
Now the exercise: 0.81

00:04:45.248 --> 00:04:49.145
0.65, 0.91

00:04:49.145 --> 00:04:53.042
0.61, 0.55

00:04:53.042 --> 00:04:55.083
0.71.

00:04:55.083 --> 00:04:58.608
There are six values, so six buckets

00:04:58.608 --> 00:05:00.557
each 1/6 wide.

00:05:00.557 --> 00:05:04.268
Which bucket does 0.65 go to?

00:05:04.268 --> 00:05:08.350
And which buckets hold more than one value?

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.180
Pause the video and work it out.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.593
Six times 0.65

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:19.266
is 3.9, rounded down

00:05:19.266 --> 00:05:20.335
bucket 3.

00:05:20.335 --> 00:05:21.709
With six buckets

00:05:21.786 --> 00:05:23.847
the bucket is not the first

00:05:23.924 --> 00:05:25.680
decimal digit any more.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:28.494
0.55 and 0.61

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:30.511
also land in bucket 3

00:05:30.585 --> 00:05:32.751
and 0.71 and

00:05:32.826 --> 00:05:34.693
0.81 in bucket 4.

00:05:34.693 --> 00:05:36.933
These values are not uniform

00:05:36.933 --> 00:05:39.024
all six are above 1/2

00:05:39.024 --> 00:05:41.638
so buckets 0 to 2 stay empty

00:05:41.638 --> 00:05:43.430
and the others fill up.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.559
What does it cost?

00:05:45.559 --> 00:05:47.280
Creating the lists

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.981
distributing and concatenating are linear.

00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:54.768
Sorting a bucket that holds n_j values with

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:58.210
insertion sort costs O(n_j²).

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.867
In the worst case all n values

00:06:00.944 --> 00:06:02.558
fall into one bucket

00:06:02.558 --> 00:06:05.480
and bucket sort takes n² time.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.781
But with uniform input

00:06:07.781 --> 00:06:11.173
each bucket holds one value on average

00:06:11.258 --> 00:06:14.396
and the expected value of n_j²

00:06:14.481 --> 00:06:16.516
is 2 - 1/n.

00:06:16.516 --> 00:06:19.230
So the expected time is linear.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.272
The buckets take linear extra space.

00:06:22.346 --> 00:06:25.337
And bucket sort is stable if each bucket

00:06:25.412 --> 00:06:27.580
is sorted with a stable sort

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:29.150
like insertion sort

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:32.140
and values are appended in input order.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.671
Counting sort is costly when

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.064
the range is large

00:06:36.133 --> 00:06:38.502
a million nine-digit numbers would

00:06:38.571 --> 00:06:40.243
need a billion counters.

00:06:40.313 --> 00:06:42.333
Radix sort sorts such numbers

00:06:42.403 --> 00:06:45.398
one digit at a time, with only ten buckets

00:06:45.398 --> 00:06:48.393
starting from the least significant digit.

00:06:48.463 --> 00:06:50.761
Each pass distributes the numbers

00:06:50.831 --> 00:06:52.294
by the current digit

00:06:52.294 --> 00:06:55.080
and collects them back in bucket order.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:57.318
Every pass must be stable

00:06:57.391 --> 00:07:00.441
numbers with the same digit keep the order

00:07:00.514 --> 00:07:02.910
they had after the previous pass.

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:06.400
A queue per bucket gives this for free

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.620
because a queue is first in, first out.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:13.598
Example 1: 170

00:07:13.598 --> 00:07:17.615
45, 75, 90, 2

00:07:17.615 --> 00:07:22.043
24, 802, 66

00:07:22.043 --> 00:07:25.338
written with three digits each.

00:07:25.338 --> 00:07:29.560
10 empty queues, numbered 0 to 9.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.247
Pass 1, by the ones digit

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.834
170 and 90

00:07:34.917 --> 00:07:36.336
go to queue 0

00:07:36.336 --> 00:07:39.674
2 and 802 to queue 2

00:07:39.674 --> 00:07:41.426
24 to queue 4

00:07:41.426 --> 00:07:44.514
45 and 75 to queue 5

00:07:44.514 --> 00:07:46.350
and 66 to queue 6.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.362
Collect the queues in order

00:07:49.362 --> 00:07:52.715
170, 90, 2

00:07:52.715 --> 00:07:57.054
802, 24, 45

00:07:57.054 --> 00:07:59.520
75, 66.

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:02.414
Pass 2, by the tens digit

00:08:02.414 --> 00:08:05.554
taking the numbers in this new order

00:08:05.554 --> 00:08:08.942
2 and 802 to queue 0

00:08:08.942 --> 00:08:12.247
24 to queue 2, 45 to queue 4

00:08:12.247 --> 00:08:15.718
66 to queue 6, 170 and

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:18.940
75 to queue 7, 90 to queue 9.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:22.684
Collect: 2, 802

00:08:22.684 --> 00:08:26.615
24, 45, 66

00:08:26.615 --> 00:08:31.310
170, 75, 90.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:34.480
Pass 3, by the hundreds digit

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:36.765
6 numbers go to queue 0

00:08:36.765 --> 00:08:39.355
170 to queue 1

00:08:39.355 --> 00:08:42.250
and 802 to queue 8.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:44.916
Collect, and the array is sorted.

00:08:44.989 --> 00:08:47.698
These three rows are exactly what the

00:08:47.771 --> 00:08:50.260
trace program in the notes prints.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.152
Look at queue 0 in Pass 3

00:08:53.152 --> 00:08:56.094
6 numbers with the same hundreds digit

00:08:56.094 --> 00:08:58.755
and they came out in the right order.

00:08:58.755 --> 00:08:59.176
Why?

00:08:59.176 --> 00:09:00.997
Because the queue kept the

00:09:01.067 --> 00:09:02.328
order of Pass 2

00:09:02.398 --> 00:09:04.499
where they were already sorted

00:09:04.569 --> 00:09:06.320
by their last two digits.

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:09.365
That is the whole proof of correctness

00:09:09.365 --> 00:09:10.470
in one picture.

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:13.510
We will write it out properly in part 4.

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:18.091
Example 2: 25, 32

00:09:18.091 --> 00:09:22.307
93, 22, 34.

00:09:22.307 --> 00:09:26.849
Pass 1, by the right digit: 32

00:09:26.849 --> 00:09:31.065
22, 93, 34

00:09:31.065 --> 00:09:32.471
25.

00:09:32.471 --> 00:09:36.363
32 stays before 22

00:09:36.363 --> 00:09:38.850
because it came first.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.160
Pass 2, by the left digit, stably

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:47.230
22, 25, 32

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:50.200
34, 93.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:52.440
22 and 25

00:09:52.512 --> 00:09:54.329
share the left digit 2.

00:09:54.402 --> 00:09:56.728
They come out in the right order

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:59.271
only because 22 was before

00:09:59.344 --> 00:10:01.306
25 after pass 1.

00:10:01.306 --> 00:10:03.777
An unstable pass could swap them

00:10:03.777 --> 00:10:06.030
and the answer would be wrong.

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:07.395
To sum up.

00:10:07.395 --> 00:10:07.787
1:

00:10:07.787 --> 00:10:11.084
The stable counting sort counts the keys,

00:10:11.084 --> 00:10:12.575
takes prefix sums,

00:10:12.575 --> 00:10:15.949
and places the records from right to left.

00:10:15.949 --> 00:10:18.304
2: BucketSort puts uniform

00:10:18.382 --> 00:10:20.030
reals into n buckets.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.620
It takes linear time on average

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:25.210
and n² in the worst case.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:26.098
3:

00:10:26.098 --> 00:10:30.044
RadixSort makes one stable pass per digit

00:10:30.044 --> 00:10:34.080
from the least significant, with 10 queues.

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:35.870
Try this yourself:

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:38.830
radix-sort 123,

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:40.670
251,

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:42.750
369,

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:44.910
278,

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.830
451,

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:49.390
222 by hand

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:52.350
and write the array after each pass.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:55.814
In part 4: RadixSort in Java,

00:10:55.814 --> 00:10:59.400
why it is correct, how long it takes,

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.420
and all 10 sorts side by side.

